<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Android Network Security Configuration for Project NEXUS

  Certificate pinning for api.project-nexus.ie: the Google Trust Services WE1
  INTERMEDIATE as the primary pin, and the GTS Root R4 as the rotation-safe
  backup. Deliberately NOT the leaf.

  🔴 Why the leaf pin was REMOVED on 2026-08-20, because re-adding it would
  recreate a live problem rather than tighten anything.

  The leaf certificate has a 90-DAY lifetime (measured: notBefore 2026-08-10,
  notAfter 2026-11-08) and rotates automatically. Pinning it therefore guarantees
  the pin goes stale about four times a year. It already had: this file claimed
  "Pins last verified: 2026-07-14" and by 2026-08-20 the pinned leaf
  (Owkg3TiAdb9cU+XKSXkJfvD2tCx+supL5btxtXNJaJE=) matched NOTHING in the live
  chain, which had rotated to Qk4GkShf/CuASLSvDeBlIeHwQ8obPwOyh0sGZ+cOZ1M=.

  The app kept working only because Android trusts a chain when ANY certificate in
  it matches ANY pin, so the intermediate pin carried it alone. That is the backup
  pin doing exactly the job it was added for — but it left the app one pin deep,
  with no spare, which is the state that bricks an app if the CA rotates.

  And the leaf pin was buying nothing. With the intermediate pinned, the effective
  constraint is already "a certificate issued by GTS WE1"; adding the leaf narrows
  nothing while guaranteeing quarterly staleness and inviting exactly the false
  confidence found above — a "last verified" date that was five weeks out of date.

  What is pinned now, and how long each is good for (measured from the live chain):

    WE1 intermediate   notAfter 2029-02-20  — primary
    GTS Root R4        a root; lifetime in decades — backup

  Two pins at different depths means an intermediate rotation cannot lock anyone
  out, which was the one remaining way this could take the app down.

  Refresh procedure: `bash scripts/get-cert-pins.sh` prints the live chain, and
  `node scripts/check-cert-pins.mjs` checks these pins against it. The 90-day
  expiry headroom is enforced by `npm run verify:release`.
-->
<network-security-config>
  <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
    <domain includeSubdomains="false">api.project-nexus.ie</domain>
    <!--
      expiration is a FAIL-OPEN safety valve: after this date Android stops
      enforcing the pin set entirely, so the app keeps working but loses pinning.
      Set ~12 months out and reviewed deliberately, rather than far enough away to
      be forgotten. `verify:release` fails once fewer than 90 days remain, so the
      gate goes red around 2027-06-03 — long before pinning silently lapses.
    -->
    <pin-set expiration="2027-09-01">
      <!-- GTS WE1 intermediate — verified 2026-08-20 from the live chain, notAfter 2029-02-20 -->
      <pin digest="SHA-256">kIdp6NNEd8wsugYyyIYFsi1ylMCED3hZbSR8ZFsa/A4=</pin>
      <!-- GTS Root R4 — verified 2026-08-20 from the live chain; survives an intermediate rotation -->
      <pin digest="SHA-256">mEflZT5enoR1FuXLgYYGqnVEoZvmf9c2bVBpiOjYQ0c=</pin>
    </pin-set>
  </domain-config>

  <!-- Default: use system trust store, no cleartext traffic permitted -->
  <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
    <trust-anchors>
      <certificates src="system" />
    </trust-anchors>
  </base-config>
</network-security-config>
